(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-beta-calcium-blockers-quiz"]={"title":"Class II & IV: Beta Blockers & Calcium Channel Blockers Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why are verapamil and diltiazem used with caution in liver damage?","choices":["They are eliminated unchanged by the kidneys","They cause hepatic dysfunction","They need loading doses","They undergo hepatic metabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem undergo hepatic metabolism, so caution is advised in liver damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Besides slowing atrioventricular node conduction, what do class IV calcium channel blockers do to automaticity?","choices":["They increase it","They decrease it","They leave it unchanged","They raise it in ischemic tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class IV drugs decrease automaticity, block the L-type channel, slow atrioventricular node conduction and are negative inotropes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Verapamil and diltiazem belong to which antiarrhythmic class?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem are the class IV calcium channel blockers; they block the L-type channel and slow atrioventricular node conduction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why is propranolol a concern in a patient with asthma?","choices":["It can cause bronchospasm","It can cause pneumonitis","It can cause respiratory paralysis","It can cause pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol is nonselective, and bronchospasm is on its toxicity list; a beta-1 selective blocker causes less. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Where are verapamil and diltiazem most effective?","choices":["In ischemic tissue","In normal ventricular muscle","In the most active tissue","In tissue at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem block both activated and inactivated channels and are most effective in the most active tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class is made up of the beta receptor antagonists?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class 2 antiarrhythmics are the beta receptor antagonists, such as propranolol, metoprolol, acebutolol and esmolol. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for metoprolol and acebutolol?","choices":["Sedation and fatigue","Seizures and nystagmus","Tinnitus and blurred vision","Neuropathy and ataxia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central nervous system sedation and fatigue are listed for metoprolol and acebutolol, with congestive heart failure. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which conduction disorder is a listed toxicity of propranolol?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Heart block","QT prolongation","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol slows atrioventricular conduction, and heart block is on its toxicity list with heart failure and hypotension. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which condition can a beta-1 selective blocker such as metoprolol still worsen?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia","Glaucoma","A seizure disorder","Congestive heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol and acebutolol cause less bronchospasm, but congestive heart failure remains on their list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What do verapamil and diltiazem do to the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes?","choices":["Suppress the sinoatrial node, slow the atrioventricular node","Speed both the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes","Hyperpolarize both nodes through potassium channels","Shorten phase 3 in both nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem suppress the sinoatrial node and decrease atrioventricular conduction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Metoprolol and acebutolol are which type of beta blocker?","choices":["Nonselective","Beta blockers that block potassium channels","Beta-1 selective","Beta blockers that block sodium channels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol and acebutolol are beta-1 selective blockers, similar to propranolol but with less bronchospasm. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which effects does propranolol have on the heart?","choices":["Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic","Negative inotropic and chronotropic","Positive inotropic and chronotropic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol has negative inotropic and chronotropic effects, slows atrioventricular conduction and reduces phase 4 activation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
